    <h3>ANOMALY T-01: <code>pulse.py</code> — TRUNCATED TELEGRAM_TOKEN (CRITICAL — UNRESOLVED)</h3>

    <p>
        Line 12 of <code>pulse.py</code> reads:
    </p>

    <pre style="background: #111; border: 1px solid #333; padding: 10px; color: #39FF14; overflow-x: auto;">
TELEGRAM_TOKEN=os.get...EN")</pre>

    <p>
        This is syntactically invalid Python. The expression <code>os.get...EN")</code> is not a
        valid function call. The ellipsis and capitalization suggest truncation — the middle of a
        line like <code>os.getenv("TELEGRAM_TOKEN")</code> was stripped, possibly by a filesystem
        interruption, an aggressive IDE auto-save collision, or a buffer flush during a write
        operation.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Impact:</strong> The Pulse Scheduler is non-functional. Every 30 minutes, GitHub
        Actions executes <code>pulse.py</code>, which attempts to load environment variables,
        fails to parse the broken <code>TELEGRAM_TOKEN</code> line, and exits silently or throws a
        <code>SyntaxError</code> before any Telegram message is sent. The self-healing scheduler
        loop described in the prior audit is dead. The heartbeat has flatlined.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Secondary anomaly (T-01a):</strong> Even if the line were syntactically valid, the
        <code>pulse.yml</code> workflow injects environment variables as
        <code>TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN</code> and <code>TELEGRAM_HOME_CHANNEL</code>, while
        <code>pulse.py</code> reads <code>TELEGRAM_TOKEN</code> and
        <code>TELEGRAM_HOME_CHANNEL</code>. The variable name mismatch on the token key means
        that even with a corrected line, the workflow would pass the wrong variable name unless
        either <code>pulse.py</code> is updated to read <code>TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN</code>, or
        <code>pulse.yml</code> is updated to inject <code>TELEGRAM_TOKEN</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Resolution:</strong> Replace line 12 with
        <code>TELEGRAM_TOKEN = os.getenv("TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN")</code> and change the environment
        reference in <code>pulse.yml</code> to match. Alternatively, update <code>pulse.yml</code>
        to inject <code>TELEGRAM_TOKEN</code> directly.
    </p>

